# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so without this the next
# checkout rewrites the tree to CRLF and anything executed on the Linux or macOS runners
# fails on an invisible character. eol=lf, not bare text=auto.
# Verify with `git ls-files --eol` and expect i/lf w/lf. Counting \r\n in PowerShell gives
# a false reading, because PowerShell normalizes line endings on its own.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.props     text
*.slnx      text
*.md        text
*.yml       text
*.json      text
*.ics       text

# Snapshot baselines are compared byte for byte. Pin them to LF so a Windows checkout does
# not silently rewrite every verified file and fail the whole suite.
*.verified.txt text eol=lf

# Binary. Never touch these.
*.png       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.ico       binary
